{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.MagnusPairingSignStructure","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MagnusPairingSignStructure","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Trace","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Notation","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.magnus_pairing_sign_indefinite","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.magnus_pairing_sign_indefinite","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MagnusPairingSignStructure.lean#L68-L79","doc":"**The escape.** The linear commutator-trace pairing is genuinely sign-indefinite: one fixed\ncommutator `⁅A,B⁆`, paired against two different densities `ρ₊, ρ₋`, gives a strictly positive\nand a strictly negative trace. So — unlike the non-negative quadrature of\n`trace_mul_transpose_nonneg` — the pairing is not sign-fixed, which is exactly why the\nsecond-order Magnus term can carry the sign the traceless `F_q` could not.\n\nWitnesses: `A = e₀₁`, `B = e₁₀` give `⁅A,B⁆ = diag(1,−1)`; then `ρ₊ = diag(1,0)` yields trace\n`+1` and `ρ₋ = diag(0,1)` yields trace `−1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.trace_mul_transpose_nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.trace_mul_transpose_nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MagnusPairingSignStructure.lean#L59-L66","doc":"**The wall.** The Frobenius quadrature `tr(F·Fᵀ) = Σᵢⱼ (F i j)²` is non-negative. A\ntraceless curvature `F_q` enters the binding only through this square, so it can never supply a\nbinding-reducing (negative) contribution — the reason the quadrature route `qou-jqof` is\nexcluded from being the missing negative-at-A=2 term. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.trace_commutator_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.trace_commutator_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":51,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MagnusPairingSignStructure.lean#L51-L57","doc":"**The `ρ = I` control.** The trace of a commutator vanishes: `tr(A·B − B·A) = 0`, by\ncyclicity of the trace. Consequently the linear Magnus pairing `tr(ρ·Ω₂)` with `ρ = I` and\n`Ω₂` a sum of commutators is identically zero — it carries no built-in sign, and is therefore\nfree (unlike a quadrature) to be negative for a suitable density. ","signatureChunk":0}]}